    "closed": true,
    "closedAt": "2024-11-12T10:43:12Z",
    "comment_count": 3,
    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author": {
                "avatarUrl": "https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/24854248?u=d09fd974c6720eba3039db6beb2f1c1efecdb51d&v=4",
                "login": "echasnovski",
                "resourcePath": "/echasnovski",
                "url": "https://github.com/echasnovski"
            },
            "authorAssociation": "MEMBER",
            "body": "I think there is some misunderstanding here.\r\n\r\n'mini.diff' has a more general model than working only with Git: it has buffer (i.e. current) text and reference text. Two of them are compared with each other whenever each of them changes. How reference text is computed depends on the buffer source. You can read more [here](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/7b4d5d48b6b5a75009d63f8f3e4ef4819b7e8139/doc/mini-diff.txt#L83).\r\n\r\nThe default source [compares buffer's text with the text in the Git index](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/7b4d5d48b6b5a75009d63f8f3e4ef4819b7e8139/doc/mini-diff.txt#L20-L21), i.e. the text in the Git staging area, i.e. staged version of the file. Here are a bit [more details](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/7b4d5d48b6b5a75009d63f8f3e4ef4819b7e8139/doc/mini-diff.txt#L528-L536).\r\n\r\n> Sometimes I skim over the changes (`]h`) and stage everything I'd like to commit (`gh`). As of now, there is no indication in the sign column, whether a hunk is staged or not. Would it be possible to have different signs or colors for modified/staged hunks?\r\n\r\nStaging hunk means adding it to the index. This means that the text version in the buffer and in the reference text are the same. As there is no difference, there is no diff hunk to act (show, navigate, etc.) upon. Hence nothing is shown (in sign/number column, in overlay, etc.)\r\n\r\n------\r\n\r\nDoes this clarify things about how 'mini.diff' works?",
            "bodyHTML": "<p dir=\"auto\">I think there is some misunderstanding here.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">'mini.diff' has a more general model than working only with Git: it has buffer (i.e. current) text and reference text. Two of them are compared with each other whenever each of them changes. How reference text is computed depends on the buffer source. You can read more <a href=\"https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/7b4d5d48b6b5a75009d63f8f3e4ef4819b7e8139/doc/mini-diff.txt#L83\">here</a>.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">The default source <a href=\"https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/7b4d5d48b6b5a75009d63f8f3e4ef4819b7e8139/doc/mini-diff.txt#L20-L21\">compares buffer's text with the text in the Git index</a>, i.e. the text in the Git staging area, i.e. staged version of the file. Here are a bit <a href=\"https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/7b4d5d48b6b5a75009d63f8f3e4ef4819b7e8139/doc/mini-diff.txt#L528-L536\">more details</a>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Sometimes I skim over the changes (<code class=\"notranslate\">]h</code>) and stage everything I'd like to commit (<code class=\"notranslate\">gh</code>). As of now, there is no indication in the sign column, whether a hunk is staged or not. Would it be possible to have different signs or colors for modified/staged hunks?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Staging hunk means adding it to the index. This means that the text version in the buffer and in the reference text are the same. As there is no difference, there is no diff hunk to act (show, navigate, etc.) upon. Hence nothing is shown (in sign/number column, in overlay, etc.)</p>\n<hr>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Does this clarify things about how 'mini.diff' works?</p>",
            "bodyText": "I think there is some misunderstanding here.\n'mini.diff' has a more general model than working only with Git: it has buffer (i.e. current) text and reference text. Two of them are compared with each other whenever each of them changes. How reference text is computed depends on the buffer source. You can read more here.\nThe default source compares buffer's text with the text in the Git index, i.e. the text in the Git staging area, i.e. staged version of the file. Here are a bit more details.\n\nSometimes I skim over the changes (]h) and stage everything I'd like to commit (gh). As of now, there is no indication in the sign column, whether a hunk is staged or not. Would it be possible to have different signs or colors for modified/staged hunks?\n\nStaging hunk means adding it to the index. This means that the text version in the buffer and in the reference text are the same. As there is no difference, there is no diff hunk to act (show, navigate, etc.) upon. Hence nothing is shown (in sign/number column, in overlay, etc.)\n\nDoes this clarify things about how 'mini.diff' works?",
